WRITERS_DEPTH["writers/learn/academic-writing"] = """
<h2>Desk notes: what academic writing actually demands</h2>
<p>Academic writing is argument under citation discipline. The form asks you to make a claim, show exactly where every supporting fact came from, and let the reader check both — which is why it feels slow at first and trustworthy once it clicks. The guides in this section walk the core moves: the thesis that says one precise thing, the paragraph that develops it, and the citation that keeps you honest.</p>
<p>For the conventions themselves, the <a href="https://owl.purdue.edu/owl/purdue_owl.html" rel="noopener">Purdue OWL</a> is the desk's standing external recommendation: its style-guide coverage (APA, MLA, Chicago) is free, maintained and authoritative in a way aggregated tips never are. Pair it with this section's guides and you have both the formal rules and the working habits.</p>
<p>The desk's one addition for writers entering academia from journalism or blogging: academic register is not stuffing your sentences with long words. It is hedging precisely ("the data suggest" rather than "this proves"), attributing every claim, and structuring for a reader who will check you. If you can write a clean reported article, you already have the muscles; the section's <a href="/writers/learn/editing-proofreading/">editing guides</a> tune them to the form.</p>"""

