WRITERS_DEPTH["writers/learn/editing-proofreading"] = """
<h2>Desk notes: two different jobs, in the right order</h2>
<p>Editing and proofreading fail when they run together, so this section insists on the sequence: revise (argument, structure, what the piece says) then edit (paragraphs, sentences, what each part does) then proofread (surface: spelling, punctuation, consistency). Doing proof-level work on a draft that still needs structural surgery is the most common wasted afternoon in writing — the guides here sequence the work so each pass has one job.</p>
<p>The desk's standing techniques, expanded: the read-aloud pass catches rhythm damage that silent reading forgives; the reverse outline (one sentence per paragraph, then read the sentences) exposes structural gaps no line edit can hide; and the print-or-change-font trick defeats your brain's autocomplete, which is the enemy in every proofing pass. The Purdue OWL's <a href="https://owl.purdue.edu/owl/purdue_owl.html" rel="noopener">writing-process and revision pages</a> formalise all three, and the <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/guardian-observer-style-guide-a" rel="noopener">Guardian style guide</a> settles most consistency arguments (numbers, titles, abbreviations) with an authoritative word.</p>
<p>And the calibration that keeps this work humane: a piece needs enough editing to be clear and honest, not enough to be inhuman. When a draft has lost your voice in the name of polish — that is a real failure mode, the opposite of the mess it was meant to fix — walk it back one pass.</p>"""

